(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl")
 (:adr/doc_type "adr")
 (:adr/topic "kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl")
 (:adr/title "KIR is defined as an IPLD codec, schema and ADL — with the checker, not the schema, as its authority")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-07")
 (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
 (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir" "kotoba-lang/io-ipld" "kotoba-lang/amu"))
 (:adr/related (vector "2608160100" "2608160200" "2608139980" "2607266000" "2607289500"))
 (:adr/body "## Context\n\nThe owner asked whether KIR is properly understood as an IPLD codec, schema and ADL. It is, and most of the machinery already exists — but the layer that hashes KIR does not go through it, and the two normalizations disagree on every byte.\n\n### What is already built (measured 2026-09-07)\n\n`kotoba-lang/io-ipld` keeps IPLD's layers distinct rather than treating CBOR as the whole stack: `ipld.data-model` (nine Data Model kinds, lossless validation, the universal INode interface), `ipld.core` (strict DAG-CBOR plus CID-verified block reads), `ipld.schema-dsl` (user-facing IPLD Schema syntax compiled to normalized Schema DMT), `ipld.schema` (Schema-Schema DMT validation, bounded representation unification, metered ADL capabilities, logical/representation projection in both directions, all six union representations), `ipld.selector` and `ipld.graph` (bounded traversal producing CAR/GraphSync proof blocks), and `kotoba.value.codec` — the language-facing facade over the canonical `kotoba.value.v1` value codec.\n\nADLs in this stack are already Kotoba artifacts. `ipld.schema/wasm-adl-capability` defines the boundary `ipld-adl-wasm-v1`: the transform module is pinned by raw CID with an explicit operation set, the trusted engine receives only canonical DAG-CBOR plus remaining fuel, maximum output bytes and memory pages, and there is no ambient filesystem, network, clock, randomness or host callback in the schema API. Its engine is `kotoba.compiler.ipld-adl` in amu (bounded Wasmtime), which additionally re-encodes the guest output and requires byte equality with canonical DAG-CBOR, because decoding alone does not separate a non-canonical encoding from a canonical one.\n\nSo defining KIR as codec/schema/ADL adds no new layer. It closes a loop: the ADL transform is itself something amu wove.\n\n### The divergence this decision exists to resolve (measured 2026-09-07)\n\n`kotoba-kir/deps.edn` does not depend on `io-ipld`. `kotoba.kir.definition-identity` requires only `cbor.core` and `multiformats.core` and hand-writes its own `normalize`, `rank` and `cmp`. The result is two independently maintained canonical encodings of the same problem, which converged on the same architecture — a closed injective tagged 2-tuple in canonical CBOR, so a keyword can never collide with the string of the same name — and share no byte:\n\n| value | KIR DefCID (payload v2) | kotoba.value.v1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| tag | string: int, f64, str, kw | integer: 2, 3, 4, 5, 9 |\n| 5 | [int 5] -> 8263696e746135 | [2 5] -> 820205 |\n| i64 max | [int 9223372036854775807] as decimal TEXT -> 8263696e747339323233333732303336383534373735383037 | [9 <8 byte BE two-s complement>] -> 8209487fffffffffffffff |\n| 1.5 | [f64 3ff8000000000000] as 16 hex TEXT -> 82636636347033666638303030303030303030303030 | [3 <8 byte float>] -> 8203483ff8000000000000 |\n| a (string) | [str a] -> 82637374726161 | [4 a] -> 82046161 |\n| :a (keyword) | [kw a] -> 82626b776161 | [5 a] -> 82056161 |\n\nBoth choices are defensible for their own stated reason. KIR carries numbers as exact text because a 64-bit literal exceeds the JavaScript exact-integer range and text keeps :clj and :cljs on the same bytes — payload v1 hashed `(pr-str canonical-edn)` while labelling the CID dag-cbor, which was wrong on both halves. `kotoba.value.v1` carries exact i64 as append-only scalar code 9 with an 8-byte big-endian payload so a BigInt never routes through CBOR's Number integer path. Neither is a mistake. The defect is that nothing forces them to agree, and `kotoba.value.codec`'s own docstring names the compiler as a consumer that should be using it.\n\nThe two layers do not even agree on what an exact i64 INPUT is. `ipld.value/int64` refuses the decimal string with `int64-not-an-exact-integer` and requires a `js/BigInt`; `kotoba.kir.definition-identity/i64` accepts whatever it is handed and stringifies it. That is not a live break — `normalize` refuses raw out-of-range integers and raw platform floats outright, and amu's `canonical-value` already detects `js/BigInt` and produces exact text before KIR sees it, which is why the JVM route and the JDK-free nbb route mint byte-identical CIDs. It is the shape of the risk: two contracts for the same scalar, enforced in two places, with one caller keeping them aligned by hand.\n\n### Precedent\n\nThis workspace has already resolved exactly this shape one layer up. The alpha-normalization walk lived in both `kotoba.compiler.definition-identity` and `kotoba.codebase.typed-code` — the same algorithm, over the same KIR, in two places, with neither one the authority. kotoba-lang `lang/code-identity.edn` recorded it as a residual risk of :ci8 and named the fix as a single owner in kotoba-kir, not a third place; it landed there 2026-09-02 as `kotoba.kir.alpha-normalization`. The DefCID encoder against `kotoba.value.v1` is the same finding, one layer down.\n\n## Decision\n\n**1. KIR's transport and identity representation is IPLD, and `io-ipld` owns it.** `kotoba-kir` gains a dependency on `io-ipld`; this is acyclic — `io-ipld` depends only on `dev-protobuf`, `io-multiformats` and `org-ietf-cbor`, and `kotoba-kir` already carries the latter two. **Refined 2026-09-07 by the measurement below**: this decision first read *the canonical bytes come from `ipld.core` / `kotoba.value.codec` rather than a second hand-written normalization*, which conflated two layers. The codec is already shared — both encoders are `(cbor/encode <tagged form>)` over the same `cbor.core` from `org-ietf-cbor`, and there is no second DAG-CBOR encoder to unify. What differs is the VALUE MODEL above it, and adopting `kotoba.value.v1` there is payload v3 rather than a refactor. Read every later use of *a second normalization* in this ADR as the value model.\n\n**2. The KIR block shape is an explicit IPLD Schema.** Written in `ipld.schema-dsl`, validated as Schema DMT by `ipld.schema/compile-schema`. Structure, references, representation tables and named-reference closure are pinned there.\n\n**3. Packed KIR representations are ADLs, through the existing boundary.** `ipld-adl-wasm-v1`, module pinned by raw CID, fuel and output-node and output-byte budgets declared by the caller, receipts recording module plus input and output CIDs. No second ADL mechanism.\n\n**4. The schema pins shape. It does not pin checkedness — and the two must be distinguishable in the output.** KIR is a CHECKED IR: native typed-feature admission, the closed scalar-variant export boundary (qualified name, 1 to 32 unique cases, :i64 or :bool payloads), typing, effect rows, lowering budget. IPLD Schema validation is structural and representational; it cannot express \"this effect row is the transitive semantic closure of the calls this definition makes\" and it has no cardinality constraints, so it cannot express the variant bound either. Therefore:\n\n- A block that validates against the KIR schema and then FAILS admission MUST NOT report the same result as one that passes both. `compile-schema` returning green is not \"valid KIR\".\n- Admission is carried the way `ipld.schema` already carries advanced representations: a caller-owned required capability that fails closed when absent, the same shape as the required `visible?` argument in `kotoba-query/bridge`. A missing checker is a refusal, never a pass.\n- This is the eight-questions class the workspace keeps hitting: a check that could not run must not return the value of a check that ran and found nothing.\n\n**5. The DefCID seals the LOGICAL form only.** Schema version and representation version MUST NOT enter the identity payload. Sealing them would move every definition's identity on a purely representational change, such as adding a union representation; not sealing them while letting the encoder vary would give one meaning two identities. The seam is the one `ipld.schema` already provides: `representation->logical!` and `logical->representation!`. The six sealed inputs named by kotoba-lang `lang/code-identity.edn` — typed KIR, profile version, desugar contract version, effect row, interface, direct definition dependencies — are unchanged by this decision.\n\n**6. Changing the bytes is a payload version bump, not a tidy-up.** If moving onto `kotoba.value.v1` changes any canonical byte, that is payload v3, announced the way v2 announced itself: v3 CIDs are deliberately not v2 CIDs. Every frozen vector in kotoba-lang `lang/code-identity-vectors.edn`, every module lock, and every compile cache key moves with it. A change presented as \"unifying the encoders\" that silently moves DefCIDs is refused.\n\n**7. kototama's program form is not KIR.** The Kototama reduction plane is a closed S-expression whose canonical wire form is tagged DAG-CBOR vectors (`spec/kototama-vm-v1.edn`, :planes :reduction). Giving KIR an IPLD Schema does not make KIR the VM's instruction form. These are two schemas at two layers — build-time IR and runtime program — and they stay two. Unifying them is a separate decision with its own evidence, and nothing runs the S-expression plane today in any case: `kotoba-vm`'s `kototama-profile.edn` lists :closed-kotoba-s-expression among the :core/v1 omissions.\n\n**8. No new repository.** `io-ipld` owns codec, schema, selector and ADL. `kotoba-kir` owns admission, the logical form and the DefCID. `amu` owns the ADL engine and the compiler-side canonicalization of scalars. Adding a ninth home for \"KIR IPLD\" would recreate the finding this ADR exists to close.\n\n## What is NOT claimed\n\n- No live incorrect CID was measured. The two encoders are reachable from one codebase and disagree; the compiler currently keeps its side exact by hand.\n- The KIR IPLD Schema is not written yet. This ADR decides that it is the form, and who owns it.\n- Nothing here is a gate yet. `verify-west-pins` has no analogue for encoder agreement, and this decision does not invent one; see next step.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n    nbb --classpath \"orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/io-ipld/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-cbor/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/io-multiformats/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/org-nist-sha2/src\" <probe>\n\nwhere the probe encodes the same logical scalar through `kotoba.kir.definition-identity/normalize` plus `cbor.core/encode` and through `ipld.value/encode-value`, and compares the hex. Read the sources at their remote main; the shared checkouts run behind.\n\n## Measured 2026-09-07 — the next step, executed\n\nThe differential test named here is landed as `kotoba.kir-value-codec-differential-test` (kotoba-kir), with `io-ipld` pinned **test-only** so the measurement does not pretend the migration happened.\n\n**All ten frozen vectors reproduce byte-for-byte through `canonical-bytes`, and all ten disagree with `kotoba.value.v1`.** So payload v3 IS needed: adopting the value model moves every DefCID, every module lock and every compile cache key. The value codec does not refuse the payload — it accepts all ten and returns different bytes — which is asserted separately so a refusal can never be miscounted as a disagreement.\n\nThe divergence is three independent dimensions, not a tag rename:\n\n| | `normalize` (here) | `value->form` (`kotoba.value.v1`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| tag alphabet | strings: `map` `kw` `set` `int` `str` | integers: `19` `5` `18` `2` `4` |\n| scalar payload | integers as exact decimal **text** | native integers, append-only scalar code for exact i64 |\n| map key order | its own `rank`/`cmp` | DAG-CBOR canonical **length-first** |\n\nNeither key order is wrong. `normalize` never emits a CBOR map at all — it emits `[map [pairs]]` — so the codec's map-ordering rule does not bind it and it had to pick one. A migration that changed only the tag alphabet would still move every CID through the other two.\n\nBoth directions were shown before the test was called landed: perturbing one frozen hex fails the golden half naming that vector, and simulating a closed gap fails the debt marker with the payload-v3 message. The full kotoba-kir suite passes with the new test dependency.\n\n## Still to decide\n\nWhether to pay payload v3 at all. The cost is now measured and the benefit — one value model instead of two — is unchanged. **This ADR does not decide it**; decision 1 says where the bytes should come from, and the price of getting there turned out to be a version bump that touches every locked identity in the workspace. That is an owner decision, and the differential test is what keeps it from happening by accident.")))
